 the last digits of each term and their product.\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b0b5b2b5f8f4202e8f6931b2fd2d6b8a728b055161ef310c93fd347a2768fc5f"},"seconds":32.7432918548584,"seed":173468078898446229,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit of the expression \\((1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102)\\), we will first consider the last digits of each term and their products.\n\n1. **Last digit of \\((1001001)(1000100)\\):**\n   - The last digit of \\(1001001\\) is \\(1\\).\n   - The last digit of \\(1000100\\) is \\(0\\).\n   - Therefore, the last digit of the product \\((1001001)(1000100)\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n2. **Last digit of \\((989899)(11102)\\):**\n   - The last digit of \\(989899\\) is \\(9\\).\n   - The last digit of \\(11102\\) is \\(2\\).\n   - Therefore, the last digit of the product \\((989899)(11102)\\) is \\(8\\) (since \\(9 \\times 2 = 18\\)).\n\nSince the first term ends in \\(0\\), the last digit of the entire expression will be determined by the last digit of the second term, which is \\(8\\).\n\nTo find the number of trailing zeros, we need to count the number of factors of \\(10\\) in the expression. A factor of \\(10\\) is composed of a factor of \\(2\\) and a factor of \\(5\\).\n\n- The term \\((1001001)(1000100)\\) has at least one factor of \\(10\\) because \\(1000100\\) itself ends in \\(0\\).\n- The term \\((989899)(11102)\\) does not contribute any trailing zeros because neither \\(989899\\) nor \\(11102\\) ends in \\(0\\).\n\nThus, the number of trailing zeros in the entire expression is \\(1\\).\n\nTherefore, the rightmost non-zero digit is \\(8\\), and it is followed by \\(1\\) zero. The ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(8, 1)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(8, 1)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit of the expression \\((1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102)\\), we will first consider the last digits of each term and their products.\n\n1. **Last digit of \\((1001001)(1000100)\\):**\n   - The last digit of \\(1001001\\) is \\(1\\).\n   - The last digit of \\(1000100\\) is \\(0\\).\n   - Therefore, the last digit of the product \\((1001001)(1000100)\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n2. **Last digit of \\((989899)(11102)\\):**\n   - The last digit of \\(989899\\) is \\(9\\).\n   - The last digit of \\(11102\\) is \\(2\\).\n   - Therefore, the last digit of the product \\((989899)(11102)\\) is \\(8\\) (since \\(9 \\times 2 = 18\\)).\n\nSince the first term ends in \\(0\\), the last digit of the entire expression will be determined by the last digit of the second term, which is \\(8\\).\n\nTo find the number of trailing zeros, we need to count the number of factors of \\(10\\) in the expression. A factor of \\(10\\) is composed of a factor of \\(2\\) and a factor of \\(5\\).\n\n- The term \\((1001001)(1000100)\\) has at least one factor of \\(10\\) because \\(1000100\\) itself ends in \\(0\\).\n- The term \\((989899)(11102)\\) does not contribute any trailing zeros because neither \\(989899\\) nor \\(11102\\) ends in \\(0\\).\n\nThus, the number of trailing zeros in the entire expression is \\(1\\).\n\nTherefore, the rightmost non-zero digit is \\(8\\), and it is followed by \\(1\\) zero. The ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(8, 1)}\\).","id":"algebra:117"}
